// Birth of the Báb: 50 questions (35 easy, 10 medium, 5 hard). Drafts and fact-check notes: drafts/birth-bab/.
// Birth, family and early life
{"type":"single","q":"In which city was the Báb born?","choices":["Tehran","Iṣfahán","Shíráz","Búshihr"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb was born in Shíráz, in southern Iran, on 20 October 1819.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"Before He announced His mission, what was the Báb's trade?","choices":["Farmer","Teacher","Merchant","Physician"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb was a young merchant in His family's trading business, known for His honesty.","source":"bahai.org — The Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"What was the Báb's given name?","choices":["Mírzá Ḥusayn-ʻAlí","Siyyid ʻAlí-Muḥammad","Mullá Ḥusayn","Muḥammad-Riḍá"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He was born Siyyid ʻAlí-Muḥammad. \"Báb\" is the title He later took.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these were members of the Báb's family? (select all)","choices":["His wife, Khadíjih Bagum","His mother, Fáṭimih Bagum","His uncle, Ḥájí Mírzá Siyyid ʻAlí","Mullá Ḥusayn"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Mullá Ḥusayn was the first to believe in the Báb, not a relative. The Báb's wife, mother and uncle were His family in Shíráz.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these describe the Báb as a young man? (select all)","choices":["He worked as a merchant","He was known for honesty","He made a pilgrimage to Karbilá","He served as a soldier"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The young Báb was a respected merchant known for honesty and reliability, and He visited the holy city of Karbilá.","source":"Wikipedia — Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Báb was born in October 1819.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb was born on 20 October 1819. Today Bahá'ís celebrate His birth on a date set by the lunar calendar, so it moves from year to year.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Báb's wife was named Khadíjih.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb married Khadíjih Bagum in Shíráz in 1842. She was among the first to believe in Him.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Báb was a descendant of the Prophet Muḥammad.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Through both parents the Báb was a Siyyid, a descendant of the Prophet Muḥammad.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Báb's schoolteacher felt the young Báb needed far more schooling.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"His schoolmaster told the family that the child had no need of teachers like himself.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these stages of the Báb's early life in order, earliest first.","items":["Joins the family business","Is born in Shíráz","Marries Khadíjih","Goes to school as a child"],"answer":[1,3,0,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He was born in 1819, went to school, joined the family business at about fifteen, and married in 1842.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"spell","clue":"The title of the Herald of our Faith, meaning \"the Gate\".","word":"Báb","difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb took this title when He announced His mission in 1844. He opened the way for the coming of Bahá'u'lláh.","source":"bahai.org — The Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"Who raised the Báb after His father died?","choices":["His grandfather","His maternal uncle","His older brother","His schoolmaster"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The Báb's father died when He was young, and His devoted maternal uncle, Ḥájí Mírzá Siyyid ʻAlí, also a merchant, raised Him.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which places were part of the Báb's life before 1844? (select all)","choices":["Shíráz","Búshihr","Karbilá","Constantinople"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"He grew up in Shíráz, worked as a merchant in the port of Búshihr, and made a pilgrimage to Karbilá in Iraq.","source":"Wikipedia — Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these events before 1844 in order, earliest first.","items":["Birth of His son, Aḥmad","Marriage to Khadíjih","Pilgrimage to Karbilá","Death of His father"],"answer":[3,2,1,0],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"His father died when He was a child. Then came the Karbilá pilgrimage (about 1841), His marriage (1842) and the birth of His son (1843).","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"What was the name of the Báb's father, a well-known cloth merchant in Shíráz?","choices":["Siyyid Káẓim","Mírzá Buzurg","Ḥájí Mírzá Áqásí","Siyyid Muḥammad-Riḍá"],"answer":3,"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"His father was Siyyid Muḥammad-Riḍá, a respected cloth merchant in Shíráz. He passed away when the Báb was a child.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
// Declaration and ministry
{"type":"single","q":"Who was the first person to recognize the Báb, on the evening of His Declaration?","choices":["Quddús","Mullá Ḥusayn","Ṭáhirih","Bahá'u'lláh"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Mullá Ḥusayn met the Báb outside the gate of Shíráz, and the Báb welcomed him as the first to believe in Him.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"Which Holy Day remembers the evening the Báb announced His mission to Mullá Ḥusayn?","choices":["Naw-Rúz","Declaration of the Báb","Day of the Covenant","Ascension of Bahá'u'lláh"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Declaration of the Báb recalls the evening of 22 May 1844 in Shíráz, when He shared His mission with Mullá Ḥusayn.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"How many followers made up the Letters of the Living?","choices":["12","19","9","18"],"answer":3,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The first eighteen believers were the Letters of the Living. Together with the Báb they number nineteen.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"Which Letter of the Living was a gifted poet who championed the equality of women and men?","choices":["Ṭáhirih","Quddús","Mullá Ḥusayn","Anís"],"answer":0,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Ṭáhirih, whose name means \"the Pure One\", is remembered for her poetry and her courage in championing the equality of women and men.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Báb declared His mission in the month of May.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Declaration to Mullá Ḥusayn took place in May 1844, which is why Bahá'ís celebrate that Holy Day in spring.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Báb made a pilgrimage to Mecca.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"After the Letters of the Living had recognized Him, the Báb travelled to Mecca and Medina with Quddús.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Mullá Ḥusayn was the last of the Letters of the Living to recognize the Báb.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Mullá Ḥusayn was the very first. Quddús is remembered as the last of the eighteen.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these were Letters of the Living? (select all)","choices":["Mullá Ḥusayn","Quddús","Ṭáhirih","Bahá'u'lláh","ʻAbdu'l-Bahá"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Mullá Ḥusayn, Quddús and Ṭáhirih were among the eighteen Letters of the Living, the Báb's first disciples.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these were fortresses where the Báb was held prisoner? (select all)","choices":["Máh-Kú","Chihríq","Haifa","ʻAkká"],"answer":[0,1],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb was confined in the mountain fortresses of Máh-Kú and then Chihríq, yet His followers kept growing in number.","source":"bahai.org — Life of the Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these events in the Báb's life in order, earliest first.","items":["Martyrdom in Tabríz","Imprisonment at Chihríq","Declaration to Mullá Ḥusayn","Pilgrimage to Mecca","Imprisonment at Máh-Kú"],"answer":[2,3,4,1,0],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb declared His mission in 1844, journeyed to Mecca, was held at Máh-Kú and then Chihríq, and gave His life in Tabríz in 1850.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"Which Letter of the Living accompanied the Báb on His pilgrimage to Mecca?","choices":["Mullá Ḥusayn","Ṭáhirih","Quddús","Anís"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Quddús, the last of the Letters of the Living, travelled with the Báb to Mecca and Medina.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these chapters of the Báb's ministry in order, earliest first.","items":["Trial in Tabríz","Pilgrimage to Mecca","Imprisonment at Máh-Kú","Time in Iṣfahán"],"answer":[1,3,2,0],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"After returning from Mecca, the Báb stayed in Iṣfahán, was then held at Máh-Kú, and was brought to Tabríz for questioning in 1848.","source":"Wikipedia — Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
// Teachings and writings
{"type":"spell","clue":"The name of the Báb's chief book, the Mother Book of His Dispensation.","word":"Bayán","difficulty":"easy","explanation":"\"Bayán\" means \"exposition\" or \"utterance\". He wrote it in Persian and in a shorter Arabic version.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Saiedi, Chronology"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Badíʻ calendar has nineteen months of nineteen days each.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb established a calendar of 19 months of 19 days, with a few extra days to complete the year. Bahá'ís still use it for the Nineteen Day Feast.","source":"Wikipedia — Teachings of the Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these are true of the Báb? (select all)","choices":["He declared His mission in 1844","He was born in Shíráz","He was the Forerunner of Bahá'u'lláh","He revealed the Kitáb-i-Aqdas"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb was born in Shíráz in 1819 and declared His mission in 1844. The Kitáb-i-Aqdas was revealed by Bahá'u'lláh.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Teachings of the Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"Whom did the Báb foretell as \"Him Whom God shall make manifest\"?","choices":["The Promised One, whom Bahá'ís recognize as Bahá'u'lláh","A future disciple of the Báb","The twelfth Imam, returned","A king of Persia"],"answer":0,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Báb spoke again and again of this coming Manifestation. Bahá'ís recognize Bahá'u'lláh as that Promised One.","source":"Wikipedia — Teachings of the Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"The Qayyúmu'l-Asmá', begun on the night of the Báb's Declaration, is a commentary on which chapter of the Qur'án?","choices":["The Súrih of Abundance (al-Kawthar)","The Súrih of Mary (Maryam)","The Súrih of the Cow (al-Baqarah)","The Súrih of Joseph (Yúsuf)"],"answer":3,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The Báb began this commentary on the Súrih of Joseph (Yúsuf) on the night of His Declaration in May 1844.","source":"Saiedi, Chronology of the Báb's Writings"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these are writings of the Báb? (select all)","choices":["Persian Bayán","Commentary on the Súrih of Joseph (Yúsuf)","Book of Names (Kitáb-i-Asmá')","Kitáb-i-Aqdas","Kitáb-i-Íqán"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The Báb wrote the Persian Bayán, the Commentary on the Súrih of Joseph (Yúsuf) and the Book of Names. The Kitáb-i-Aqdas and Kitáb-i-Íqán were revealed by Bahá'u'lláh.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"order","q":"Teaser: put these writings of the Báb in the order they were written, earliest first.","items":["Persian Bayán","Commentary on the Súrih of Joseph (Yúsuf)","Book of Names (Kitáb-i-Asmá')","Kitáb-i-Fihrist (list of early writings)"],"answer":[1,3,0,2],"difficulty":"hard","time":30,"explanation":"The Commentary on Joseph began on the night of the Declaration (1844), the Fihrist listed His works in 1845, the Persian Bayán came at Máh-Kú, and the Book of Names near the end of His time at Chihríq.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Saiedi, Chronology of the Báb's Writings"}
{"type":"single","q":"How many chapters does the Qayyúmu'l-Asmá' have?","choices":["19","111","361","202"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"hard","time":25,"explanation":"The Qayyúmu'l-Asmá', the Báb's commentary on the Súrih of Joseph (Yúsuf), has 111 chapters.","source":"Saiedi, Chronology of the Báb's Writings"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which works were largely written while the Báb was held at Máh-Kú? (select all)","choices":["The Persian Bayán","The Arabic Bayán","The Seven Proofs","The Qayyúmu'l-Asmá'"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"hard","time":30,"explanation":"Most of the Persian Bayán, the Arabic Bayán and the Seven Proofs were written in Máh-Kú. The Qayyúmu'l-Asmá' dates from 1844 in Shíráz.","source":"Saiedi, Chronology of the Báb's Writings"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the last unity (váḥid) of the Persian Bayán has only ten gates instead of nineteen.","answer":true,"difficulty":"hard","time":25,"explanation":"The Persian Bayán has nine unities and breaks off at the tenth gate of the ninth, left unfinished in anticipation of Him Whom God shall make manifest.","source":"Saiedi, Chronology; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
// Shrine, legacy and the Twin Holy Birthdays
{"type":"single","q":"What colour is the dome of the Shrine of the Báb?","choices":["Blue","Golden","White","Green"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The golden dome is covered with about 12,000 gilded tiles and shines above the gardens of Haifa.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of the Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"In which city is the Shrine of the Báb located?","choices":["Tehran","Shíráz","Jerusalem","Haifa"],"answer":3,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Shrine stands on Mount Carmel in Haifa, in the Holy Land.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of the Báb"}
{"type":"spell","clue":"The mountain in Haifa where the Shrine of the Báb stands.","word":"Carmel","difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Shrine of the Báb rests on the slope of Mount Carmel, looking out over the Mediterranean.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of the Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Shrine of the Báb is surrounded by terraced gardens.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Nineteen garden terraces stretch from the foot of Mount Carmel up to its brow, with the Shrine at their heart.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of the Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: ʻAbdu'l-Bahá laid the Báb's remains to rest on Mount Carmel.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"ʻAbdu'l-Bahá built a tomb on Mount Carmel and laid the Báb's remains there in 1909.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Shrine of the Báb"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Báb and Bahá'u'lláh met in person.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"They never met face to face, though they corresponded. The Báb's writings are full of love for Him Whom God shall make manifest.","source":"Shoghi Effendi, God Passes By"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Bahá'ís celebrate the Birth of the Báb and the Birth of Bahá'u'lláh on two days in a row.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"These are the Twin Holy Birthdays, celebrated on consecutive days each autumn according to the lunar calendar.","source":"Universal House of Justice, 10 July 2014"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these describe the Báb's role? (select all)","choices":["Forerunner of Bahá'u'lláh","Herald of a new Manifestation of God","Successor to ʻAbdu'l-Bahá","Bearer of a divine Revelation in His own right"],"answer":[0,1,3],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Bahá'ís see the Báb as a Manifestation of God who prepared the way for Bahá'u'lláh, \"Him Whom God shall make manifest\".","source":"Wikipedia — Báb; bahai.org"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these moments in the Báb's story in order, earliest first.","items":["His remains are laid to rest on Mount Carmel","He is born in Shíráz","He declares His mission to Mullá Ḥusayn","He is martyred in Tabríz"],"answer":[1,2,3,0],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Born in 1819, He declared His mission in 1844, was martyred in 1850, and was laid to rest on Mount Carmel in 1909.","source":"Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these steps in the journey of the Báb's remains in order, first to last.","items":["Carried by sea to ʻAkká","Laid to rest on Mount Carmel","Secretly rescued and hidden in Persia","Taken in secret through Baghdad and Damascus"],"answer":[2,3,0,1],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The remains were rescued after 1850, hidden for decades, carried to ʻAkká in 1899 and laid to rest on Mount Carmel in 1909.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb; Wikipedia — Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"Who designed the golden-domed superstructure of the Shrine of the Báb, built under Shoghi Effendi?","choices":["Fariborz Sahba","Charles Mason Remey","William Sutherland Maxwell","Louis Bourgeois"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Shoghi Effendi asked the Canadian Bahá'í architect William Sutherland Maxwell to design the superstructure, completed in 1953. The original six-room building was raised under ʻAbdu'l-Bahá.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of the Báb"}
{"type":"single","q":"In which year did the Báb's remains arrive by sea at ʻAkká?","choices":["1850","1891","1899","1909"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The remains reached ʻAkká in 1899 and were laid to rest on Mount Carmel ten years later, in 1909.","source":"Wikipedia — Báb; Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
{"type":"spell","clue":"The Báb is called the ___ of Bahá'u'lláh: the One who came before and prepared the way.","word":"Forerunner","accept":["Fore-runner"],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Shoghi Effendi described the Báb as the Forerunner of One more mighty than Himself.","source":"Bahaipedia — The Báb"}
